      <div class="prose">
        <div class="abstract"><h2>Abstract</h2>
          <p>Risk desks answer questions that no single volatility number can answer, and they answer them by revaluing positions against <em>joint paths</em>. CASE, the Conditional Asset Scenario Engine, is a conditional diffusion model over multi-asset return panels, built as one reusable path generator rather than a model of one statistic. Asset identity enters through observable characteristics instead of fixed name slots, attention is factorised into an asset pass and a time pass, and continuing an observed history is an ordinary denoising step. One frozen checkpoint is re-conditioned each morning and never refitted, and the paper scores its risk <em>statement</em> against what actually happened, across 139 rolling decisions and nine book compositions.</p>
          <div class="kw"><b>Keywords</b> generative models · conditional diffusion · multi-asset scenario generation · portfolio risk measurement · market risk · transformer</div>
        </div>
        <p>This page summarises a manuscript under review. The full method, experiments and results are in the paper; code, checkpoints and a preprint follow the editorial decision.</p>

        <h2 id="bW">Where the idea came from</h2>
        <p>Generative models learn a distribution and, underneath it, a representation. That is what made them interesting to me as an undergraduate working on diffusion models for images and for imbalanced data: the sample is the visible part, the representation is what carries over. When I moved to finance the obvious application was market simulation — generate scenarios, revalue a book against them, read off its risk. The obvious application also has an obvious problem. A market scenario is not one object; it is the joint path of many assets, and that path is shaped by a <i>massive</i> number of factors at once — which names are in the book, what regime the market is in, how volatile each name has been, what the cross-section looked like this morning. A generator that tries to learn all of that unconditionally learns an average market, and the average market is the one you never have to make a decision in.</p>
        <p>So the question became: what has to be conditioned on, and what can be left to the model? The answer I settled on is that the generator should be a <strong>conditional surrogate</strong> rather than a world model — a single learned object that is told the state of the world it is being asked about (the book, the names, the recent window) and produces only the paths relevant to that state. Conditioning is what shrinks the space of paths from "every market" to "this market, this morning, this book". And if asset identity enters as observable characteristics rather than as fixed slots, the same surrogate can answer for names and universes it never saw, because the representation it learned is of characteristics, not tickers.</p>
        <h3 id="bW-2">From representation to a market twin</h3>
        <p>The engineering follows from that framing. Attention is split into an asset pass and a time pass, so the model represents "which names move together" separately from "how a path unfolds", and the two can be inspected separately. Continuing an observed history is treated as an ordinary denoising step, so the same checkpoint that generates from scratch can also extend what the desk has already seen. The result is less a simulator of the market than a <strong>market twin</strong> for the purposes of one decision: a surrogate that is re-conditioned each morning, never refitted, and asked a question it was not trained on.</p>
        <h3 id="bW-3">Why the experiment is about cost, not realism</h3>
        <p>A generated path can look right and still be useless, and I spent long enough admiring samples to learn that the hard way. The experiment that eventually made sense scores the engine by the decision it feeds: for a set of portfolio books across years of decision dates, how much capital does the engine say to hold, and how often does the realised loss exceed it? That is the practical cost a scenario generator is for — a reserve, a shortfall, a book revalued — and it is where a surrogate earns or fails to earn its place next to the estimators a desk already runs. The configuration was frozen before the later decisions were seen, so the confirmatory part of the evaluation is exactly that, and the paper reports where the accuracy stops as carefully as where it holds.</p>
        <p>The companion project <a href="/research/tailflow/">TailFlow</a> asks the same question on a synthetic market where the true tail risk is known, so that "the decision held" can be checked against "the decision was right".</p>

        <h2 id="m"><span class="num">1</span>The method in brief</h2>
        <p>Each return is split into a slowly moving volatility state and a whitened shape, so the market state is explicit in the representation and the same representation carries across frequencies. Each asset enters as six observable characteristics rather than a ticker slot, so no parameter depends on the number of assets: a name the model never saw is a new input, not a new model. The denoiser alternates attention across assets and across days, and every day carries its own noise level, so observed days are simply days at noise level zero and one set of weights both generates from scratch and continues a history. Volatility, value at risk, expected shortfall and drawdown are all read from the same generated paths, so the four numbers cannot contradict one another.</p>
